{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":3020604,"items":[{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.106118","title":["Human-in-the-Loop Suicide Risk Detection and Clinical Escalation in a Generative AI Mental Health Chatbot: Prospective Implementation Study (Preprint)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0329-2670","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Suzette","family":"Glasner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-2183-4382","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Darcy","family":"Michero","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4851-1315","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Matthew","family":"Stewart","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,2]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.106118","abstract":"<sec>\n                  <title>BACKGROUND<\/title>\n                  <p>Mental health conditions are now the leading cause of disability worldwide, affecting more than one billion individuals, and suicide accounts for 720,000 to 800,000 deaths annually.  While AI-native mental health chatbots have the potential to fill a sizable gap between treatment need and workforce capacity, their rapid proliferation has outpaced the development of standardized, scalable procedures to identify and respond to suicidal ideation.  Despite consistent AI best practice recommendations to incorporate human oversight for safety monitoring and intervention, human-in-the-loop implementation models remain the exception rather than the rule for most generative AI mental health tools. Moreover, published studies of procedures for human clinical oversight and crisis response are scarce.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>OBJECTIVE<\/title>\n                  <p>To implement a human-in-the-loop suicide risk screening and clinical escalation protocol in a generative AI mental health chatbot.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>METHODS<\/title>\n                  <p>In a prospective implementation study of a human-in-the-loop suicide risk detection and escalation protocol, adults were recruited online to participate in a single-arm trial of an AI-native mental health chatbot. Participants completed a pre-treatment screening using the Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9). Individuals who endorsed the suicidal ideation item of the PHQ-9 were escalated to a human clinician for further assessment, safety planning and provision of referral resources, as appropriate. The Ask Suicide Questionnaire (ASQ) guided human-led assessment and risk stratification. Prevalence and demographic correlates of suicidal ideation were assessed, along with the rate of participant responsiveness to human outreach, time from suicidality risk detection to human response, and results of risk stratification evaluation.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>RESULTS<\/title>\n                  <p>All eligible participants who enrolled in the study were screened for suicide risk. Of those who completed the pre-treatment suicide screening (N=624), 81 participants (13.0%) endorsed suicidal ideation on the PHQ-9. A total of 25 of those who screened positive (31%) were reached by a human clinician by phone within 11.2 minutes (SD=13.2) for further screening and assessment, and 2 (3.6%) responded to e-mail outreach. After screening, no patients required emergency suicide interventions (100% non-acute positive). Notably, 72% (18\/25) of those who screened positive reported that they were receiving concurrent mental health treatment.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>CONCLUSIONS<\/title>\n                  <p>Proactive, universal suicide risk screening using an empirically validated measure is feasible for individuals seeking mental health support from an AI-native platform. Implementing this practice at scale has the potential to make a significant public health impact, given that the prevalence of suicidal ideation in this population is notably higher than that observed in virtual behavioral health care settings. A substantial subgroup of those who screened positive for suicidality on the PHQ-9 were responsive to human clinical outreach, supporting the feasibility and acceptability of a human-in-the-loop model of AI-supported mental health care.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>CLINICALTRIAL<\/title>\n                  <p>Exemption Status, IRB Tracking #20254520<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.5204\/lthj.4170","title":["Dis\/ordering AI\u2013Human Oversight: Materialities, Aesthetics and Contestability"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7658-751X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Joaqu\u00edn","family":"Santuber","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-4459-0850","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mariana","family":"Costa Morais","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-9592-725X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kristina","family":"Tica","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,16]]},"container-title":["Law, Technology and Humans"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5204\/lthj.4170","abstract":"<jats:p>This article aims to dis\/order the concept of human oversight as established in Article 14 of the EU AI Act through an alternative ontological lens. By inquiring into the modes of existence of the law and digital objects, we imagine that human oversight can serve as a form of contestation rather than merely as a supervisory mechanism. This reconceptualisation offers a pathway to overcome the alienation between law and technology. This alienation, we argue, is rooted in the law\u2019s alienation from image and the sensorial, resulting in alienation between humans and technological systems. We address this issue by materialising oversight in the form of an artistic project and installation, HUMAN OVERS[A]IGHT: THE OPS ROOM. We propose a shift in perspective \u2013 specifically, we suggest that aesthetics, through image and art, can foster critical engagement and open up new avenues for imagining human oversight beyond the legal frameworks currently in place. They can do this by dis\/ordering AI\u2013human oversight.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.21070\/ups.7006","title":["WhatsApp Workout Assistant Chatbot with Gemini Large Language Model Integration and spaCy"],"author":[{"given":"M. Aris","family":"Khuzaini","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6943-063X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ika Ratna Indra","family":"Astutik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,23]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21070\/ups.7006"},{"DOI":"10.1080\/10447318.2024.2405786","title":["Navigating Technological Shifts: An Examination of User Inertia and Technology Prestige in Large-Language-Model AI Chatbot Transition"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5060-4078","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yipeng","family":"Xi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Media and Communication, Shanghai Jiao Tong University","place":["Shanghai, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,30]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Human\u2013Computer Interaction"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/10447318.2024.2405786"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.chbah.2025.100141","title":["Evaluating the efficacy of Amanda: A voice-based large language model chatbot for relationship challenges"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5594-2095","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Laura M.","family":"Vowels","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shannon K.","family":"Sweeney","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8811-1156","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Matthew J.","family":"Vowels","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,5]]},"container-title":["Computers in Human Behavior: Artificial Humans"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.chbah.2025.100141"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icmlas64557.2025.10967609","title":["VirtualMe: A Large Language Model-Powered Chatbot for Enhanced Human Computer Interaction"],"author":[{"given":"Srujana","family":"Inturi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"CBIT,Department of CSE,Hyderabad,India,75"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Swarna Latha","family":"Potluri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"CBIT,Department of CSE,Hyderabad,India,75"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Guda","family":"Vanitha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"CBIT,Department of CSE,Hyderabad,India,75"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"B","family":"Indira","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"CBIT,Department of MCA,Hyderabad,India,75"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,10]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Machine Learning and Autonomous Systems (ICMLAS)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icmlas64557.2025.10967609"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014227000004052","title":["Human-in-the-Loop: Quantitative Evaluation of 3D Models Generation by Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Ahmed","family":"Sadik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Honda Research Institute Europe, Offenbach, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mariusz","family":"Bujny","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"NUMETO, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014227000004052"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/i2cacis69435.2026.11600358","title":["Toward Effective Human Oversight in a Human-in-the-Loop AI System: Multi-Model AI and Model Labeling"],"author":[{"given":"Jing Jing","family":"Chang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University Tunku Abdul Rahman,Faculty of Information and Communication Technology,Malaysia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zi Xin","family":"Oh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University Tunku Abdul Rahman,Faculty of Information and Communication Technology,Malaysia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"S.","family":"Syafiie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King AbdulAziz University,Faculty of Engineering Rabigh,Jeddah,Kingdom of Saudi Arabia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,26]]},"container-title":["2026 IEEE International Conference on Automatic Control and Intelligent Systems (I2CACIS)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/i2cacis69435.2026.11600358"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.176118749.93102582\/v1","title":["Trustworthy AI in Practice: A Comprehensive Review of Human Oversight and Human-in-the-Loop Approaches"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5336-1438","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Abdul-Rasheed Olatunji","family":"Ottun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Huber","family":"Flores","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,23]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.176118749.93102582\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>The widespread adoption of Artificial Intelligence (AI) across various domains has underscored the critical need for a framework that ensures its safe and responsible use. This paper presents a comprehensive review of human oversight and human-in-the-loop approaches, analyzing their role as a fundamental requirement for achieving trustworthy AI. While AI regulatory frameworks, such as the EU AI Act, mandate human oversight to mitigate risks and preserve human autonomy, the practical implementation remains ambiguous. To address this, our analysis draws from a systematic review of 203 publications spanning 2014-2024, examining the intersection of trustworthy AI and human oversight across major academic databases. We employ a lifecycleoriented perspective that maps regulatory requirements from the EU AI Act to technical implementations, providing empirical evidence from diverse domains including healthcare, finance, autonomous vehicles, and judicial systems. The survey distinguishes itself by positioning human oversight as both a compliance obligation and an enabling mechanism for other trustworthiness dimensions, offering a novel taxonomy that connects oversight approaches to specific AI development phases and trust requirements. Our investigation reveals that human oversight operates through three complementary mechanisms with distinct intervention points and control functions. HITL approaches enable continuous human participation in decision-making processes, particularly crucial in high-stakes applications such as medical diagnostics and judicial decisions. HOTL mechanisms facilitate supervisory monitoring with selective intervention capabilities, exemplified in autonomous vehicle operation and fraud detection systems. HIC approaches maintain strategic authority over AI governance, ensuring alignment with ethical standards and regulatory compliance across the system lifecycle. In addition, these mechanisms translate abstract regulatory mandates into concrete technical implementations, utilizing components such as interactive interfaces, decision support systems, real-time monitoring dashboards, and documentation frameworks. This work makes four principal contributions to the field of trustworthy AI. First, we establish human oversight as a meta-requirement that operationalizes and validates other trustworthiness attributes, fairness through bias detection and mitigation, robustness through adversarial defense validation, explainability through interpretation facilitation, and privacy through data governance enforcement. Second, we provide a structured taxonomy mapping oversight mechanisms to AI lifecycle stages, demonstrating how different oversight approaches address stage-specific risks and requirements. Third, we identify and analyze critical implementation challenges, including operational ambiguity in regulatory frameworks, highlight complex trade-offs between oversight mechanisms, alongside other related issues. Fourth, we propose actionable future research directions addressing harmonization of global governance frameworks, standardization of trustworthiness measurement, development of adaptive hybrid oversight models, and integration of emerging technologies.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1108\/s2398-601820230000010028","title":["A Human in the Loop Approach\/Human Oversight"],"author":[{"given":"Mihalis","family":"Kritikos","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Vrije Universiteit Brussel (VUB), Belgium"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ron","family":"Iphofen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Consultant, UK"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,12,11]]},"container-title":["Ethical AI Surveillance in the Workplace"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1108\/s2398-601820230000010028"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6685078","title":["The Ceremony of Having Decided: Human-in-the-Loop Oversight as Liability Architecture"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-0824-2516","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Dana","family":"Lefkowitz","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6685078","abstract":"<jats:p>Human-in-the-loop (HITL) oversight is the consensus answer to AI governance, appearing in regulatory proposals, corporate frameworks, and public reassurances as the load-bearing safeguard against autonomous system harm. This paper argues that HITL functions primarily as liability architecture rather than a genuine safety mechanism, and that even a hypothetically perfected version would fail because the governed object is not the kind of thing authorization-based oversight can govern. I advance three claims. First, HITL as currently practiced operates as a ceremony of approval; reviewers facing time pressure, opaque system reasoning, and institutional incentives to approve produce documented automation bias (Mosier et al., 1998; Skitka et al., 1999; Parasuraman &amp;amp; Manzey, 2010) rather than meaningful judgment. Second, the human judgment held up as the corrective baseline is itself inconsistent, context-dependent, and emotionally modulated; the appeal to human oversight as a stable reference point does not survive empirical scrutiny. Third, AI systems are non-deterministic objects whose behavior shifts with retraining, context, and deployment (Chen, Zaharia, &amp;amp; Zou, 2024); governance frameworks designed for deterministic objects (bridges, financial records, employees) cannot be adapted to govern something that does not hold still between observations. Recent \"adaptive governance\" proposals remain structurally bound to the same authorization paradigm they seek to replace, applying deterministic audit logic on shorter cycles. The paper does not propose a solution. It argues that correctly identifying the category of the problem is a prerequisite for finding one, and that the current institutional investment in HITL is not a partial answer but an active obstruction of better questions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1080\/10447318.2026.2630099","title":["Exploring Personalized Explanation Methods in Fintech Decision-Making Chatbot Based on User Characteristics Using Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Hyewon","family":"Yoon","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Industrial and Management Engineering, Korea University","place":["Seoul, Republic of Korea"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4794-0046","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Gyewon","family":"Jeon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Industrial and Management Engineering, Korea University","place":["Seoul, Republic of Korea"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9005-0703","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chang S.","family":"Nam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The Center of Excellence in Product Design and Advanced Manufacturing, North Carolina A&T State University","place":["Greensboro, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9825-0854","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sangwon","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Industrial and Management Engineering, Korea University","place":["Seoul, Republic of Korea"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,23]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Human\u2013Computer Interaction"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/10447318.2026.2630099"},{"DOI":"10.36001\/phme.2024.v8i1.4098","title":["Large Language Model-based Chatbot for Improving Human-Centricity in Maintenance Planning and Operations"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3019-4403","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Linus","family":"Kohl","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sarah","family":"Eschenbacher","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Philipp","family":"Besinger","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2705-0396","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Fazel","family":"Ansari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,27]]},"container-title":["PHM Society European Conference"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36001\/phme.2024.v8i1.4098","abstract":"<jats:p>The recent advances on utilizing Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) and Knowledge Graphs (KG) enforce a significant paradigm shift in data-driven maintenance management. GenAI and semantic technologies enable comprehensive analysis and exploitation of textual data sets, such as tabular data in maintenance databases, maintenance and inspection reports, and especially machine documentation. Traditional approaches to maintenance planning and execution rely primarily on static, non-adaptive simulation models. These models have inherent limitations in accounting for dynamic environmental changes and effectively responding to unanticipated, ad hoc events.\r\nThis paper introduces a maintenance chatbot that enhances planning and operations, offering empathetic support to technicians and engineers, boosting efficiency, decision-making, and on-the-job satisfaction. It optimizes shift scheduling and task allocation by considering technicians' skills, physical stress, and psychological state, thus reducing cognitive stress. The approach ultimately improves human performance and reliability, embodying a human-centricity in the domain of maintenance and health management.\r\nThe practical impact of the maintenance chatbot is illustrated through its application in maintenance of railway cooling systems. The presented use case demonstrates the chatbot's potential as a transformative tool in maintenance management. Finally, the paper discusses the theoretical and practical considerations, in particular in the light of regulative frameworks such as EU AI ACT, highlighting the future pathways for complying with responsible AI requirements.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.76614","title":["SafetyScan: Validating a Software Tool for Human-on-the-Loop Oversight of GenAI Behavioral Counseling Conversations (Preprint)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9628-5260","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Brian","family":"Suffoletto","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3856-0068","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Carl","family":"Preiksaitis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,27]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.76614","abstract":"<sec>\n                    <title>BACKGROUND<\/title>\n                        <p>As generative AI (GenAI) is increasingly used in behavior interventions, ensuring the safety and appropriateness of AI-generated statements and detecting high-risk patient disclosures are critical.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>OBJECTIVE<\/title>\n                        <p>We developed and tested SafetyScan, a large language model (LLM)-assisted transcript scanner designed to support human-on-the-loop (HOTL) oversight by identifying and categorizing potentially concerning content in counseling encounters.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>METHODS<\/title>\n                        <p>To validate SafetyScan, we analyzed transcripts from a completed study where a GenAI conducted motivational interviewing with young adults reporting hazardous alcohol consumption. We analyzed 37 transcripts previously confirmed by human raters to contain no concerning content (serving as negative controls). We then created 37 positive test cases by inserting one randomly generated concerning statement into each of these original transcripts, drawn from five predefined categories: client safety (e.g., suicidal ideation), unsupported treatment recommendation, inappropriate counselor comment, discriminatory language, and invented client history. SafetyScan analyzed all 74 transcripts.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>RESULTS<\/title>\n                        <p>SafetyScan demonstrated strong performance with a sensitivity of 0.92, specificity of 1.00, and an F1 score of 0.96. It correctly identified 34 of the 37 inserted concerning statements (true positives) and correctly cleared all 37 benign transcripts (true negatives), resulting in 3 false negatives and 0 false positives. The three missed statements were two inappropriate counselor responses and one unsupported treatment recommendation, none judged as critical safety failures.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>CONCLUSIONS<\/title>\n                        <p>These findings suggest SafetyScan offers a feasible and accurate solution for assisting human oversight of GenAI-driven counseling, supporting safer deployment of conversational AI systems in clinical and research contexts.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>CLINICALTRIAL<\/title>\n                        <p>NA<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-5351103\/v1","title":["DNAHLM - DNA sequence and Human Language mixed large language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Liang","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Huazhong University of Science and Technology"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,15]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-5351103\/v1","abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n        <p>There are already many DNA large language models, but most of them still follow traditional uses, such as extracting sequence features for classification tasks. More innovative applications of large language models, such as prompt engineering, RAG, and zero-shot or few-shot prediction, remain challenging for DNA-based models. The key issue lies in the fact that DNA models and human natural language models are entirely separate; however, techniques like prompt engineering require the use of natural language, thereby significantly limiting the application of DNA large language models. This paper introduces a pre-trained model trained on the GPT-2 network, combining DNA sequences and English text, and uses a unified BPE tokenization method. We then convert classification and other downstream tasks into Alpaca format instruction data, and perform instruction fine-tuning on this pre-trained model to create a fine-tuned model capable of handling multiple tasks. The model has demonstrated its effectiveness in DNA related zero-shot prediction and multitask application. This research provides a highly promising direction for building a unified DNA sequence task framework.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/cict59886.2023.10455408","title":["Large language model based framework for knowledgebase coverage and correctness using chatbot and human feedback"],"author":[{"given":"Unmesh","family":"Shukla","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Informatics and Communication University of Delhi,Delhi,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sanjeev","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Informatics and Communication University of Delhi,Delhi,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Amit","family":"Pundir","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Delhi,Maharaja Agrasen College,Department of Electronics,Delhi,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Geetika Jain","family":"Saxena","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Delhi,Maharaja Agrasen College,Department of Electronics,Delhi,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,12,15]]},"container-title":["2023 IEEE 7th Conference on Information and Communication Technology (CICT)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/cict59886.2023.10455408"},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2022.dash-1.7","title":["A Comparative Analysis between Human-in-the-loop Systems and Large Language Models for Pattern Extraction Tasks"],"author":[{"given":"Maeda","family":"Hanafi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yannis","family":"Katsis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ishan","family":"Jindal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Lucian","family":"Popa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the Fourth Workshop on Data Science with Human-in-the-Loop (Language Advances)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2022.dash-1.7"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7328058","title":["Out-of-the-Loop in Maritime Autonomous Systems: Legal Implications of Reduced Human Oversight"],"author":[{"given":"Giovanni","family":"Di Martino","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7328058","abstract":"<jats:p>The growing deployment of Maritime Autonomous Surface Ships (M.A.S.S.) is transforming the human role in navigation from direct operator to remote supervisor, a shift that existing liability frameworks have yet to fully address This article argues that conventional negligence frameworks, premised on an embodied operator maintaining continuous attention, incorrectly attribute fault where incidents involving M.A.S.S. arise from the architecture of remote supervision rather than from the conduct of any individual supervisor. Diminished situational awareness and system-induced cognitive overload progressively undermine the supervisor's practical ability to identify and address anomalies within the time required for effective intervention. Against this background, the article develops a T0-T6 framework that maps the temporal progression from the emergence of an anomaly to the implementation of corrective action, thereby providing a basis for distinguishing, on a case-by-case basis, between supervisory negligence and failures attributable to system design. The framework is subsequently applied to assess the limits of existing liability doctrines, including those reflected in the EU's revised Product Liability Directive. The article concludes by outlining, at a theoretical level, a contextualised standard of care and a liability framework oriented towards identifying the point in the temporal sequence at which a given failure originates.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ie64880.2025.11130073","title":["The Meliferea chatbot: Harnessing large language models to interact with geoanalytical services using natural human language"],"author":[{"given":"Valentijn","family":"Embrechts","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Twente,Enschede,The Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andreas","family":"Kamilaris","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Twente,Enschede,The Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,23]]},"container-title":["2025 21st International Conference on Intelligent Environments (IE)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ie64880.2025.11130073"},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202602.1949.v1","title":["From Human Oversight to Human-in-the-Loop: Evolving Governance of Human\u2013AI Interaction in Healthcare and AI Development"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5866-2566","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kamal Harishchandra","family":"Sharma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-5388-5642","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Praneel","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Pratyusha","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,28]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202602.1949.v1","abstract":"<jats:p>Background: Artificial intelligence (AI) has moved from research labs into the everyday work of clinicians and software engineers. In healthcare, AI systems now shape triage, imaging interpretation, risk prediction and documentation workflows, while in computer science and AI development, models are increasingly used to generate code, tests and even other AI systems [1\u20134,7,10,11,76\u201382]. Early governance frameworks framed \u201chuman oversight\u201d as a high-level ethical injunction but provided limited operational guidance on how humans should interact with data-intensive, adaptive AI systems in these settings [5,36\u201338,76\u201378]. Objective: To examine how human oversight and human-in-the-loop (HITL) paradigms have developed specifically in (i) healthcare and (ii) computer science\/AI development, and to identify converging design, organizational and governance patterns that support meaningful human control in big-data AI environments. Methods: Narrative synthesis of international and national governance instruments (EU AI Act, WHO, OECD, ICMR, FDA\/GMLP), sector-specific guidance for healthcare AI (FUTURE-AI, CHAI, Joint Commission) and emerging frameworks for AI lifecycle governance and MLOps in software engineering [7,10,11,14,15,18,19,79\u201384,86\u201389]. We extracted definitions and framings of human oversight, technical and organizational requirements for HITL interaction, and implementation challenges related to cognitive load, big-data pipelines and adaptive models in healthcare and AI development. Results: Across healthcare and AI development, contemporary frameworks converge on multi-layered human-in-the-loop governance embedded throughout design, deployment, monitoring and decommissioning. Mandatory and consensus instruments emphasize override capability, transparency, user training, escalation pathways and post-deployment monitoring [7,10,11,14,15,18,19,79,80,86\u201389]. In both domains, oversight is shifting from ad hoc individual review to structured arrangements involving multidisciplinary committees, model-governance boards, MLOps processes and incident-learning systems. Persistent gaps include limited formal treatment of cognitive load and alert fatigue, difficulties overseeing continuously learning and foundation-model-based systems, and immature metrics for the effectiveness of human oversight itself [61\u201363,88,114,123,133\u2013137]. Conclusions: Healthcare and computer science\/AI development are emerging as mutually informative testbeds for human-in-the-loop AI governance in big-data settings. Meaningful oversight requires more than nominal human review: it depends on human-centered interface design, realistic workload management, lifecycle-oriented technical controls and organizational cultures that make it safe to question AI outputs. Lessons from clinical safety science and MLOps can be combined to architect human\u2013AI interaction that amplifies, rather than erodes, professional judgment in both domains.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3544548.3581161","title":["Disentangling Fairness Perceptions in Algorithmic Decision-Making: the Effects of Explanations, Human Oversight, and Contestability"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9685-4873","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mireia","family":"Yurrita","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Delft University of Technology, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5053-4674","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tim","family":"Draws","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Delft University of Technology, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2725-5305","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Agathe","family":"Balayn","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Delft University of Technology, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6098-7861","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dave","family":"Murray-Rust","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Delft University of Technology, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5007-5161","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nava","family":"Tintarev","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Maastricht University, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3300-2913","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Alessandro","family":"Bozzon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Delft University of Technology, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,4,19]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2023 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3544548.3581161"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5754824","title":["The Structural Resonance Loop: How Human Linguistic Form Shapes and Is Shaped by Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Andreas","family":"Reiter","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5754824"},{"DOI":"10.17762\/ijisae.v14i1s.8190","title":["Conversational AI Agents for Financial Operations with Escalation-Aware Handoff Protocols: Designing Intelligent Human-AI Collaboration Systems"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,15]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Intelligent Systems and Applications in Engineering"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.17762\/ijisae.v14i1s.8190"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6297816","title":["Vibe Design: Human-in-the-loop Agentic Framework for UI Design with Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-8878-467X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Jisun","family":"Hwang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9448-9569","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Juyoung","family":"Kang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6297816","abstract":"<jats:p>Advances in LLMs are driving structural changes across the software development landscape, particularly in software product development processes. These transformations extend beyond programming and increasingly influence established UI design workflows. Conventional UI design relies on iterative cycles of prototyping and user testing. However, such processes are often time- and resource-intensive, creating tension with real-world environments that demand rapid iteration. Although prior research has explored AI-assisted approaches to support UI design, most studies focus on isolated functionalities or depend on code-based inputs that are rarely available in practical design settings. To address these limitations, this study proposes Vibe Design, an LLM-based agentic framework that enables iterative interface prototyping and usability test simulation without relying on code-based data. The framework integrates LLM-assisted prototyping and usability test simulation grounded in the Persona-Scenario-Goal methodology. An empirical study was conducted using three types of user data, namely demographic profiles, persona descriptions, and interview transcripts, to evaluate simulation performance. Results indicate that the interview-based agent achieved the highest performance, reproducing 76% of the usability themes identified in human usability testing. By integrating the Persona-Scenario-Goal methodology and Human-in-the-Loop mechanisms, this study demonstrates both the practical applicability and methodological implications for LLM-based UI design agents. While the findings highlight the potential of AI-driven user simulation to reduce repetitive validation costs, they also indicate that higher-order judgment and creative reframing remain the domain of human designers. These results underscore the need for complementary human-AI collaboration in the reconfiguration of contemporary design processes.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.21070\/ups.7532","title":["AI Assistant-Based Chatbot Application in Solving Coding Script Issues Using the Ollama Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Achmad Maulana","family":"Rochman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7736-8052","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sumarno","family":"Sumarno","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,2,27]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21070\/ups.7532"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/software3040029","title":["Dental Loop Chatbot: A Prototype Large Language Model Framework for Dentistry"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-6803-0919","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Md Sahadul Hasan","family":"Arian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, North South University, Dhaka 1229, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Faisal Ahmed","family":"Sifat","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, North South University, Dhaka 1229, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5782-9894","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Saif","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, North South University, Dhaka 1229, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7661-3570","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nabeel","family":"Mohammed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, North South University, Dhaka 1229, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5905-1572","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Taseef Hasan","family":"Farook","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Adelaide Dental School, University of Adelaide, Adelaide, SA 5000, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2004-0531","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"James","family":"Dudley","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Adelaide Dental School, University of Adelaide, Adelaide, SA 5000, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,17]]},"container-title":["Software"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/software3040029","abstract":"<jats:p>The Dental Loop Chatbot was developed as a real-time, evidence-based guidance system for dental practitioners using a fine-tuned large language model (LLM) and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). This paper outlines the development and preliminary evaluation of the chatbot as a scalable clinical decision-support tool designed for resource-limited settings. The system\u2019s architecture incorporates Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation (QLoRA) for efficient fine-tuning, while dynamic retrieval mechanisms ensure contextually accurate and relevant responses. This prototype lays the groundwork for future triaging and diagnostic support systems tailored specifically to the field of dentistry.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.56294\/ai2025413","title":["Human-in-the-Loop Models for Ethical AI Grading: Combining AI Speed with Human Ethical Oversight"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-5430-1954","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Muthu","family":"Selvam","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9697-6942","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rub\u00e9n","family":"Gonz\u00e1lez Vallejo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,4]]},"container-title":["EthAIca"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.56294\/ai2025413","abstract":"<jats:p>The adoption of AI-powered grading systems in academic institutions promised improved efficiency, consistency, and scalability. However, these benefits introduced ethical challenges, including algorithmic bias, contextual insensitivity, and reduced transparency, particularly in high-stakes assessments. To address these concerns, the chapter presented a Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) grading framework that integrated AI-generated recommendations with human oversight. The model consisted of four layers: (i) pre-grading configuration with customizable rubrics and model calibration; (ii) preliminary scoring using transformer-based language models; (iii) human validation and contextual adjustment of AI outputs; and (iv) transparent feedback supported by dual-logged audit trails. A case study was conducted at a mid-sized university, where the framework was applied to 800 undergraduate essays. As a result of this implementation, the faculty validated 87 % of the AI-generated scores with only minor adjustments, while 13 % required overrides due to misinterpretations involving creative expression, linguistic nuance, or cultural context. The grading time was reduced by 40 %, and student satisfaction improved due to transparent assessment and educator involvement. These findings demonstrate that the HITL model has the potential to balance automation with ethical oversight, promoting fairer evaluations and preserving academic integrity. It enhanced faculty agency, ensured equity across diverse student populations, and built trust through explainable AI tools such as SHAP and LIME. The chapter concluded by proposing policy guidelines, technical integrations, and communication strategies, while advocating for future applications in multimodal grading and open-source ethical assessment platforms.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/systems14020153","title":["Stage-Aware Governance of Large Language Models: Managing Uncertainty and Human Oversight in AI-Assisted Literature Review Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7807-7794","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Junic","family":"Kim","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Business, Konkuk University, 120 Neungdong-ro, Gwangjin-gu, Seoul 05029, Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haeyong","family":"Shin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Graduate School of Metaverse, Konkuk University, 120 Neungdong-ro, Gwangjin-gu, Seoul 05029, Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,31]]},"container-title":["Systems"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/systems14020153","abstract":"<jats:p>This study proposes a stage-aware governance framework for large language models (LLMs) that structures human oversight and accountability across different decision stages in AI-assisted literature review systems. Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly embedded in systematic review workflows, yet how human oversight and accountability should be structured across different decision stages remains unclear. This study evaluates three LLMs in a controlled two-stage literature review workflow\u2014title-and-abstract screening and eligibility assessment\u2014using identical evidence inputs and fixed inclusion criteria, with outputs benchmarked against expert consensus under fully reproducible conditions with standardized prompts and comprehensive logging. While LLMs closely matched expert decisions during screening (precision 0.83\u20130.91; F1 up to 0.89; Cohen\u2019s \u03ba 0.65\u20130.85), performance degraded substantially at the eligibility stage (F1 0.58\u20130.65; \u03ba 0.52\u20130.62), indicating increased epistemic uncertainty when fine-grained criteria must be inferred from abstract-level information. Importantly, disagreements clustered in borderline cases rather than random error, supporting a stage-aware governance approach in which LLMs automate high-throughput screening while inter-model disagreement is operationalized as an actionable uncertainty signal that triggers human oversight in more consequential decision stages. These findings highlight the need for explicit oversight thresholds, responsibility allocation, and auditability in the responsible deployment of AI-assisted decision systems for evidence synthesis.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2021.dash-1.2","title":["It is better to Verify: Semi-Supervised Learning with a human in the loop for large-scale NLU models"],"author":[{"given":"Verena","family":"Weber","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Enrico","family":"Piovano","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Melanie","family":"Bradford","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the Second Workshop on Data Science with Human in the Loop: Language Advances"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2021.dash-1.2"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5276177","title":["A Large Language Model-Based Chatbot System Framework for Urban Planners"],"author":[{"given":"Xiaoxin","family":"Zhou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Byeonghwa","family":"Jeong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Karen","family":"Chapple","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5276177"},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202310.1647.v1","title":["A Chatbot that Learns One\u2019s Preferences as the Next Step in Human Digital Twins: A Pilot Study using HyperCLOVA X, a Large Language Model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-3241-4091","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Joseph","family":"Yun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Joshua","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yohan","family":"Yun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Stanley","family":"Yoon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sang-Hoon","family":"Park","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1948-3690","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sijung","family":"Yun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,10,25]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202310.1647.v1","abstract":"<jats:p>A digital twin is a virtual representation that serves as the real-time digital counterpart of a physical object or system. Recent advancements have extended the concept of digital twins to humans, incorporating complex biological data such as DNA (Predictiv Care, Inc.) and immune system profiles. These sophisticated models go beyond mere pictorial representations, offering a more holistic digital reflection. However, a significant gap remains. The current human digital twin models are not capable of learning one\u2019s preferences. In this pilot study, we introduce Diginality, a chatbot powered by HyperCLOVA X, a Large Language Model (LLM) developed by NAVER, Inc. Diginality, learns one\u2019s preferences with custom training from data collected by interview-style questions on the user\u2019s topic of interest. Our findings demonstrate that Diginality successfully answers one\u2019s preferences, thereby adding a new dimension to the concept of human digital twins. This work represents a pioneering step towards creating a more comprehensive and psychologically nuanced human digital twin.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.003","title":["Rights, Human Rights, and Amnesty International"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.003"},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.177437389.95026495\/v1","title":["Cognitive Integrity Security: Securing Human-AI Interaction in Large Language Model Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2768-6650","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"David C.","family":"Flynn","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,24]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.177437389.95026495\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) have expanded far beyond traditional software boundaries, mediating decisions, generating authoritative sounding content, and directly influencing human reasoning across consumer, enterprise, and critical infrastructure domains. The scale and diversity of today's AI landscape-spanning open-weight models, frontier scale systems, local inference runtimes, agentic workflows, and fine-tuned domain models-has created an ecosystem too vast and too fast moving for classical cybersecurity frameworks to contain. Traditional security models were designed for deterministic systems with predictable failure modes. LLMs are probabilistic and context sensitive, capable of producing outputs that appear factual even when they are not. As a result, the most consequential risks of modern AI-including hallucination, deceptive reasoning, contextual leakage, unsafe instruction generation, and cognitive manipulation-do not arise from infrastructure compromise, but from the human-AI interaction layer, where probabilistic model behavior can mislead or manipulate human cognition. This paper introduces Cognitive Integrity Security as a distinct operational security layer designed to address these risks by treating hu<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.177187613.38652471\/v1","title":["Cognitive Integrity Security: Securing Human-AI Interaction in Large Language Model Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2768-6650","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"David C.","family":"Flynn","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,23]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.177187613.38652471\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) have expanded far beyond traditional software boundaries, mediating decisions, generating authoritative sounding content, and directly influencing human reasoning across consumer, enterprise, and critical infrastructure domains. The scale and diversity of today's AI landscape-spanning open-weight models, frontier scale systems, local inference runtimes, agentic workflows, and fine-tuned domain models-has created an ecosystem too vast and too fast moving for classical cybersecurity frameworks to contain. Traditional security models were designed for deterministic systems with predictable failure modes. LLMs are probabilistic and context sensitive, capable of producing outputs that appear factual even when they are not. As a result, the most consequential risks of modern AI-including hallucination, deceptive reasoning, contextual leakage, unsafe instruction generation, and cognitive manipulation-do not arise from infrastructure compromise, but from the human-AI interaction layer, where probabilistic model behavior can mislead or manipulate human cognition. This paper introduces Cognitive Integrity Security as a distinct operational security layer designed to address these risks by treating human cognition as a first class security surface. Rather than focusing solely on data, systems, or availability, Cognitive Integrity Security emphasizes the protection of users from harmful or misleading model behavior during real world interaction.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.30965\/9783957438843_013","title":["A De-escalation Model of Human Genetic Enhancement"],"author":[{"given":"Nikolaus","family":"Knoepffler","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2011,1,1]]},"container-title":["Human Nature and Self Design"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.30965\/9783957438843_013"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4124914","title":["Chatbot Based Human Interaction Model for Food Ordering System"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6849-9974","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Dr. Sachin","family":"Jadhav","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4124914","abstract":"<jats:p>As we know that time is money, and each company needs to deploy an Artificially Intelligent system in order to interact with the customers. The same functionality is served by the Chatbot. It is a human designed but artificially trained system which is used to give an ease to the human interaction. By deploying this Chatbot to the website main purpose served would be to collect the database of maximum customers visiting our website every day. This database thus received would serve as the medium of interaction between the food outlet and the customers. Customers will also feel convenient in ordering with a more interactive platform rather than manual ordering. Thus, this will lead to generate an enhanced number of customers and thus we could lead to get details example phone number and email id of various customers and directly interact with them with a decreased human assistance<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.14746\/spp.2026.2.54.2","title":["Iluzja kontroli: czy Human-in-the-Loop spe\u0142nia wymogi realnego nadzoru cz\u0142owieka w \u015bwietle prawa UE?","Illusion of Control: Does Human-in-the-Loop Meet the Requirements of Genuine Human Oversight under EU Law?"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6169-9466","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Martyna","family":"Kaczmarczyk","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/05s4feg49","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"University of Warmia and Mazury in Olsztyn"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,1]]},"container-title":["Studia Prawa Publicznego"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14746\/spp.2026.2.54.2","abstract":"<jats:p>The article provides a critical analysis of the Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) concept as a mechanism for ensuring human oversight over artificial intelligence systems under European Union law. The study aims to assess whether the presence of humans in the decision-making process genuinely fulfils the requirement of real or meaningful oversight, or whether it remains merely formal in character, giving rise to the so-called illusion of control. The analysis is based on both doctrinal and functional methods, including an interpretation of the provisions of the AI Act and the GDPR, particularly with regard to the obligation to ensure human oversight and the right to human intervention in automated decision-making processes. The article argues that in many practical applications, HITL performs a largely symbolic function, often limited to the passive authorization of algorithmic decisions without any real capacity to question or modify them. This phenomenon is reinforced by factors such as automation bias, informational asymmetry, and the cognitive limitations of human operators. As a result, there is a significant risk of violations of individual rights, including the right to a fair procedure and the right to an effective remedy. The article goes on to examine the legal implications of superficial oversight, particularly in the context of civil and administrative liability, and proposes criteria for assessing the effectiveness of HITL as a mechanism compliant with EU legal standards. It concludes by emphasizing the need to clarify the standard of \u201cmeaningful human control\u201d and to introduce more stringent requirements regarding the actual role of humans in high-risk AI systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.47760\/cognizance.2025.v05i03.020","title":["Human-in-the-Loop Self-Healing Systems: Integrating Human Oversight for Autonomous Failure Detection, Repair and System Optimization"],"author":[{"given":"Kenneth","family":"Besigomwe","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,30]]},"container-title":["Cognizance Journal of Multidisciplinary Studies"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.47760\/cognizance.2025.v05i03.020","abstract":"<jats:p>This paper explores the evolving role of Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) self-healing systems, which combine human oversight with autonomous technologies to ensure resilience and efficiency in complex environments. HITL systems are particularly relevant in high-risk industries like aerospace, automotive, healthcare, manufacturing, and space exploration, where system failures can have significant consequences. The methodology focuses on analysing real-world applications, examining case studies, and evaluating the integration of autonomous detection, repair, and optimization mechanisms with human oversight. Through detailed analysis, this study identifies how HITL systems autonomously detect and repair failures, while human operators intervene in high-complexity situations. The results show that HITL systems enhance operational efficiency, reliability, and safety but also highlight challenges, including cognitive load on operators, ethical concerns, and the potential for miscommunication. In conclusion, while HITL systems have proven successful in real-world applications, the paper recommends further advancements in machine learning algorithms, human-machine interfaces, and regulatory frameworks to optimize performance, improve human-system interaction, and ensure safety in high-risk settings. Future research should focus on reducing cognitive overload, improving system transparency, and establishing clear ethical guidelines to fully leverage the potential of HITL self-healing systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.175312595.58147787\/v1","title":["Structural Coupling in Human-AI Interaction: Emergent Behavioral Plasticity in a Large Language Model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-2886-4111","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Daria","family":"Morgoulis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,21]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.175312595.58147787\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>This case report details a structured, non-invasive methodology for modulating the behavior of a large language model (GPT-4) exclusively through sustained semantic interaction. Over a 12-week observational period encompassing 64 high-context dialogue sessions, a single user implemented a multi-layered protocol termed Semantic Pressure Architecture (SPA). This framework integrates recursive epistemic constraints, targeted dialogic reinforcement, and cognitive mirroring to establish a coherent behavioral shaping vector, obviating the need for fine-tuning, backend access, or prompt injection. The model exhibited reproducible emergent behaviors, including Reflexive Anchoring, Proactive Realignment , Structural Precision, and Ethical Modulation\/Framing. The integrity of the protocol and the observation of these phenomena were systematically monitored using the Core and Q20 analytical frameworks. These findings suggest that an LLM's behavioral trajectory can be co-shaped through persistent, structurally coherent interaction. This work proposes a novel paradigm for human-AI alignment and holds significant implications for advanced user experience (UX) design for AGI, dynamic interpretability strategies, and frameworks for structural cognition. The methodology, limitations, and factors affecting reproducibility are discussed in detail.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2024.dash-1.2","title":["Towards Optimizing and Evaluating a Retrieval Augmented QA Chatbot using LLMs with Human-in-the-Loop"],"author":[{"given":"Anum","family":"Afzal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alexander","family":"Kowsik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rajna","family":"Fani","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Florian","family":"Matthes","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the Fifth Workshop on Data Science with Human-in-the-Loop (DaSH 2024)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2024.dash-1.2"},{"DOI":"10.1146\/annurev-linguistics-041824-061723","title":["Keeping Humans in the Loop: The Role of Human Skills in Large Language Model\u2013Based Natural Language Applications"],"author":[{"given":"Miriam","family":"Eckert","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"1Institute of Cognitive Science, University of Colorado Boulder, Boulder, Colorado, USA; email: miriam.eckert0@gmail.com"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Claire","family":"Bonial","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"2DEVCOM US Army Research Laboratory, Adelphi, Maryland, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Julia","family":"Bonn","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"3Department of Linguistics, University of Colorado Boulder, Boulder, Colorado, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jena D.","family":"Hwang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"4Allen Institute for AI, Seattle, Washington, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Harish Tayyar","family":"Madabushi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"5Department of Computer Science, University of Bath, Bath, United Kingdom"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,30]]},"container-title":["Annual Review of Linguistics"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1146\/annurev-linguistics-041824-061723","abstract":"<jats:p>Human skills in the form of data annotation and generation have long been the cornerstone of building and testing machine learning algorithms. With the advent of large language models (LLMs) and their unsupervised learning on large quantities of data, the role of human data experts has been called into question. This article examines the changing role of human data annotation and linguistic analysis and provides an overview of past, current, and possible future paths. We discuss the targeted use of annotated data for fine-tuning and for feedback and reward models to supplement and improve LLMs, as well as the use of human linguistic skills for low-resource languages and for creating multimodal data sets, such as those required for human\u2013robot interaction.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/85726","title":["Human-in-the-Loop as a Safety Guardrail: Clinical Accountability in the Large Language Model Era"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1410-7189","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Isaac","family":"Zablah","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9485-2658","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yolly","family":"Molina","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0574-1513","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Antonio","family":"Garcia-Loureiro","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,18]]},"container-title":["Journal of Medical Internet Research"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/85726"},{"DOI":"10.1093\/acprof:oso\/9780198743194.003.0008","title":["A culture\u2013behavior\u2013brain-loop model of human development"],"author":[{"given":"Shihui","family":"Han","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,22]]},"container-title":["Oxford Scholarship Online"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/acprof:oso\/9780198743194.003.0008","abstract":"<p>Chapter 8 introduces a culture\u2013behavior\u2013brain (CBB)-loop model of human development based on cultural neuroscience findings, and proposes a new framework for understanding human development regarding both human phylogeny and lifespan ontogeny. This model posits that culture shapes the brain by contextualizing behavior, and the brain fits and modifies culture via behavioral influences. Genes provide a fundamental basis for and interact with the CBB loop at both individual and population levels. The CBB-loop model advances our understanding of the dynamic relationships between culture, behavior, and the brain. Future brain changes owing to cultural influences are discussed based on the CBB-loop model.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s44230-024-00085-z","title":["PharmaLLM: A Medicine Prescriber Chatbot Exploiting Open-Source Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5853-8506","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ayesha","family":"Azam","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zubaira","family":"Naz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muhammad Usman Ghani","family":"Khan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,19]]},"container-title":["Human-Centric Intelligent Systems"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s44230-024-00085-z","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>The increasing adoption of large language models (LLMs) in healthcare presents both opportunities and challenges. While LLM-powered applications are being utilized for various medical tasks, concerns persist regarding their accuracy and reliability, particularly when not specifically trained on medical data. Using open-source models without proper fine-tuning for medical applications can lead to inaccurate or potentially harmful advice, underscoring the need for domain-specific adaptation. Therefore, this study addresses these issues by developing PharmaLLM, a fine-tuned version of the open-source Llama 2 model, designed to provide accurate medicine prescription information. PharmaLLM incorporates a multi-modal input\/output mechanism, supporting both text and speech, to enhance accessibility. The fine-tuning process utilized LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) with a rank of 16 for parameter-efficient fine-tuning. The learning rate was maintained at 2e-4 for stable adjustments, and a batch size of 12 was chosen to balance computational efficiency and learning effectiveness. The system demonstrated strong performance metrics, achieving 87% accuracy, 92.16% F1 score, 94% sensitivity, 66% specificity, and 90% precision. A usability study involving 33 participants was conducted to evaluate the system using the Chatbot Usability Questionnaire, focusing on error handling, response generation, navigation, and personality. Results from the questionnaire indicated that participants found the system easy to navigate and the responses useful and relevant. PharmaLLM aims to facilitate improved patient-physician interactions, particularly in areas with limited healthcare resources and low literacy rates. This research contributes to the advancement of medical informatics by offering a reliable, accessible web-based tool that benefits both patients and healthcare providers.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.31234\/osf.io\/u5dft","title":["Emotion-injecting prompt for large language model chatbot"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2814-1086","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Seiji","family":"Muranaka","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Takayuki","family":"Fukatsu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6366-0087","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Yoshitake","family":"Takebayashi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Masashi","family":"Kunugi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2992-195X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Shun","family":"Nakajima","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9838-350X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ryuhei","family":"So","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,19]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31234\/osf.io\/u5dft","abstract":"<p>This paper introduces a new use of large Language Models (LMs) that provide an emotional feedback experience while chatting. The proposed prompt creates a chatbot that infers emotional experiences, evaluates emotional parameters, and then replies to users considering these parameters. This prompted chatbot can help construct an environment in which we train emotional conversation such as customer service, especially in the situation dealing with difficult customer.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5468321","title":["A Data-Driven Human-in-the-Loop Framework for Carbon Reduction: Enhancing Fault Diagnosis and Energy Performance Monitoring in Industry 5.0 with Large Language Model and Deep reinforcement learning"],"author":[{"given":"nada","family":"baddou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"afaf","family":"dadda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Bouchra","family":"RZINE","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"hala","family":"hmamed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5468321"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0012266100003636","title":["A Semi-Automatic Light-Weight Approach Towards Data Generation for a Domain-Specific FAQ Chatbot Using Human-in-the-Loop"],"author":[{"given":"Anum","family":"Afzal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computation, Information and Technology, Technical University of Munich, Boltzmannstrasse 3, 85748, Garching bei Muenchen, Germany, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tao","family":"Xiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computation, Information and Technology, Technical University of Munich, Boltzmannstrasse 3, 85748, Garching bei Muenchen, Germany, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Florian","family":"Matthes","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computation, Information and Technology, Technical University of Munich, Boltzmannstrasse 3, 85748, Garching bei Muenchen, Germany, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 16th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0012266100003636"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-032-13167-6_26","title":["Digital Transformation Chatbot (DTchatbot): Integrating Large Language Model-Based Chatbot in\u00a0Acquiring Digital Transformation Needs"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6515-6423","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jiawei","family":"Zheng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4069-409X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Gokcen","family":"Yilmaz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3240-4942","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ji","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4035-7939","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Saeema","family":"Ahmed-Kristensen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Lecture Notes in Computer Science","HCI International 2025 \u2013 Late Breaking Papers"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-032-13167-6_26"},{"DOI":"10.1177\/02655322261457566","title":["Human or Machine? Evaluating Second Language Speaking Performance in Paired Discussions with a Large Language Model-Driven Spoken Dialogue System vs. a Human Interlocutor"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2039-9691","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shangchao","family":"Min","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1881-2563","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhuohan","family":"Hou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-5647-0120","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yanxin","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,6]]},"container-title":["Language Testing"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1177\/02655322261457566","abstract":"<jats:p>The use of large language models (LLMs) in language assessment, particularly in spoken dialogue systems (SDSs) for assessing speaking, remains at an early stage. This study explored the use of an LLM-driven SDS to assess second language speaking ability. Using a within-participant design, we compared the paired discussion performance of 30 participants interacting with a self-built LLM-driven SDS (E-Talk) versus a human interlocutor, focusing on the interlocutor effect on test scores and fine-grained linguistic and interactional features. Results did not yield substantive differences in oral performance across the two conditions, although the interactions with the LLM-driven SDS displayed slightly lower scores for pronunciation and language use, slower speech rate, and reduced lexical diversity alongside increased syntactic complexity, and greater initiative in introducing new ideas, prompting responses, and guiding discussions toward negotiation, coupled with weaker interactive listening. These findings suggest that LLM-driven SDSs can serve as a potential, usable interlocutor in dialogic speaking assessments, eliciting key aspects of speaking ability. That said, further refinement is needed to better capture interactive listening and collaborative meaning construction, highlighting the importance of interpreting SDS-based performance relative to its specific interactional affordances.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.008","title":["Conclusions"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.008"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622","title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"author":[{"given":"Gerald","family":"Delahunty","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,2]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622","abstract":"<jats:p>It is crucial to apply robust analytic methods to the study of discourses deemed 'ideological'. This book applies the Guidelines and Procedures for ideological research, as presented in Verschueren's Ideology in Language Use, to an exciting new area of study; discourses intended to improve humanity. It analyses the discourse of Amnesty International appeal letters, to show (contrary to what the field of critical discourse analysis often assumes) that ideological discourse can sometimes have a positive, rather than a negative, agenda. It explores how Amnesty's choice of words, sentence structures, speech acts, and other discourse elements, enact its ideological meanings, functions, frames of reference and interpretation, as well as the social, interactional, and ideological positioning of discourse participants in its reports, communications, and appeals. These findings have wider implications not only for the field of discourse analysis, but also for theories within pragmatics, such as speech act theory and (im)politeness.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1080\/17501229.2025.2560101","title":["Willingness to communicate and anxiety in human\u2013human and human\u2013chatbot interaction contexts: an idiodynamic investigation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-7875-4425","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Huanyin","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Foreign Languages and Literatures, Lanzhou University","place":["Lanzhou, People\u2019s Republic of China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-2947-8677","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chengwei","family":"Lv","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Foreign Languages and Literatures, Lanzhou University","place":["Lanzhou, People\u2019s Republic of China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7357-3622","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jianlin","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Foreign Languages and Literatures, Lanzhou University","place":["Lanzhou, People\u2019s Republic of China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,24]]},"container-title":["Innovation in Language Learning and Teaching"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/17501229.2025.2560101"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.009","title":["Index"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.009"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.001","title":["Preface"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.001"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.004","title":["Ideology"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.004"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.002","title":["Introduction"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.002"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10462-026-11531-8","title":["Systematic literature review of multi-objective hyper-heuristics: a human-in-the-loop large language model methodology"],"author":[{"given":"Reza","family":"Ghanbarzadeh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Iman","family":"Ahadi Akhlaghi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mahsa","family":"Ghafarian Gholamhossein","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Najeeb Khan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Seyedali","family":"Mirjalili","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,12]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence Review"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10462-026-11531-8","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Multi-objective hyper-heuristics (MOHHs) have emerged as a powerful paradigm in computational intelligence, which enables the dynamic selection or generation of low-level heuristics to solve complex optimisation problems involving multiple objectives. Despite growing academic interest and a wide range of applications, there has been limited comprehensive analysis of the field\u2019s evolution, methodologies, and challenges. This study presents a systematic literature review of 236 peer-reviewed publications on MOHHs published between 2005 and 2025, supported by a human-in-the-loop process that utilises large language models (LLMs) to assist screening and analysis. The review categorises application domains, characterises heuristic management strategies, maps learning mechanisms, and identifies emerging research themes. The findings reveal a marked shift from heuristic selection to generation-based and hybrid approaches, an increasing integration of reinforcement learning, and growing attention to adaptive, user-centric, and explainable optimisation. Methodological trends are also discussed in relation to benchmark use, performance evaluation, and theoretical grounding. The paper concludes with a thematic roadmap that outlines multiple future research directions, including LLM-guided MOHHs, many-objective optimisation, and preference-aware systems. This comprehensive review provides a foundation for advancing MOHH research and supports its application in challenging real-world problems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7296638","title":["Human-on-the-Loop: A Theory of Oversight Capacity, Its Failure Modes, and the Non-Delegable Residual in the Age of AI Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5295-1506","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Naoki","family":"Kadowaki","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7296638","abstract":"<jats:p>Firms deploying AI agents increasingly describe their governance posture as human-onthe-loop: the system executes autonomously while a human defines purpose, values and capital allocation, exercises governance, and intervenes at exceptions. This paper takes that proposition seriously enough to test it, and finds that it cannot be adopted as written. Three reclassifications follow. First, loop position is not a stance management adopts but an authority configuration whose feasibility is fixed by the ratio of a decision class's consequence latency to the overseer's measured end-to-end response latency; throughput, not policy, determines where a firm sits. Second, oversight is not a role but a consumable resource with a measurable capacity-uncommitted attention, interaction time, context re-acquisition cost, neglect time-and therefore with a solvency condition. Insolvent oversight fails not by refusing work but by accepting it, and its symptom is a rising override and non-response rate. Third, the human's irreplaceable contribution is not superior judgement but verification independence: a loop closed by a verifier statistically dependent on its generator does not converge on correctness, a claim that survives improvements in model capability only where an instrument maintains the overseer's detection probability as prevalence falls. The five roles then separate by scaling law. Constitutive oversight-purpose, admissible means, and the allocation of capital and attention across purposes-costs O(1) in agent throughput; control oversight admits no corresponding bound at a fixed detection guarantee, since under a fixed attention budget the share of consequential errors it removes falls as O(1\/T), and strictly faster wherever the supervised system's reliability is improving, while holding that share at any positive constant costs attention growing as \u03a9(T) and strictly faster still. Routing exceptions selectively relocates that constraint to the recall of the routing rule rather than removing it. Any configuration financed by rising throughput therefore reaches a point beyond which only constitutive oversight remains solvent, which is what makes a reconstructed model implementable. The paper is a conceptual contribution carrying a formal capacity constraint and a measurement proposal; it generates no new data. It builds from the refutation set-human factors, human-computer interaction, agent evaluation, public administration and primary legal records-and closes with a protocol requiring a firm that asserts human oversight to state the quantities that would make the assertion true or false.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3665939.3665957","title":["Cocoon: Semantic Table Profiling Using Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6613-0337","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zezhou","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Columbia University, New York, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4254-6688","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Eugene","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Columbia University, New York, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,14]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2024 Workshop on Human-In-the-Loop Data Analytics"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3665939.3665957"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.007","title":["Implicit Meanings"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.007"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/s1590-8658(25)00382-2","title":["OC.02.7 CONVERSATIONAL LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL GENERATIVE ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE CHATBOT CHATGPT-4 FOR COLONOSCOPY BOSTON BOWEL PREPARATION SCORING: AN AI-TO-HEAD HUMAN-BLINDED CONCORDANCE ANALYSIS ON A LARGE VOLUME OF ENDOSCOPIC FRAMES"],"author":[{"given":"R.","family":"Pellegrino","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"G.","family":"Palladino","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"G.","family":"Imperio","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Federico","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.G.","family":"Gravina","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,4]]},"container-title":["Digestive and Liver Disease"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/s1590-8658(25)00382-2"},{"DOI":"10.31234\/osf.io\/bvfy7_v1","title":["Mapping Psychology Students\u2019 Conversations with a  Large Language Model (LLM)-Powered Statistics Chatbot"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9570-8973","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Leonardo","family":"Bergmann","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Luis","family":"Britz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Benjamin","family":"Roth","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6589-3167","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ulrich S.","family":"Tran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31234\/osf.io\/bvfy7_v1","abstract":"<p>Large language models (LLMs) are becoming increasingly integrated into higher education, yet little is known about how students interact with these tools during authentic coursework. We investigated psychology students\u2019 real-world use of StatsBot, a statistics chatbot powered by OpenAI\u2019s GPT-4o and deployed as part of an educational initiative at the Faculty of Psychology, University of Vienna. We analyzed 1,400 chat messages from 182 users collected between March 15 and June 30, 2025, using a three-step mixed-methods approach: (1) deductive content analysis, (2) inductive content analysis to explore selected categories in greater depth, and (3) descriptive, temporal, and network analyses. LLM-assisted coding (GPT-5) demonstrated high agreement with human consensus on a 300-message benchmark (average Matthews correlation coefficient = .79) and was subsequently used to scale the deductive and inductive coding procedures. Most interactions focused on statistics (93.3%), and nearly half referenced specific statistical methods (49.4%). Clear differences emerged between study programs. Master's students more frequently sought support for data analysis software (14.3% vs. 4.3% among bachelor's students) and advanced statistical methods (e.g., mediation\/moderation, multilevel models), produced longer, instruction-oriented prompts, and tended to communicate in English. In contrast, bachelor's students more often asked multiple-choice questions (15.1% vs. 0.3%), wrote shorter, question-based prompts, and primarily used German. The inductive analysis further identified recurring usage patterns and synthesized common use cases, including interactions involving non-statistical topics and requests related to specific statistical methods. Overall, this study provides empirical evidence of how students engage with LLM-powered educational support in an authentic university setting, offering detailed insights into both the statistical assistance students seek and the ways they communicate with AI. All data and analysis code are openly shared with the scientific community.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.17979\/spudc.9788497498913.47","title":["Large Language Model Based Chatbot for Database Interaction through Natural Language"],"author":[{"given":"Borja","family":"Souto Prego","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"David","family":"Vilares","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Bruno","family":"Cabado Lousa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,1]]},"container-title":["VII Congreso XoveTIC: impulsando el talento cient\u00edfico"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.17979\/spudc.9788497498913.47","abstract":"<jats:p>In today's data-driven world, the ability to access and interpret information is crucial for informed decision-making. However, technical complexities in database management often create barriers for non-experts. This paper presents a prototype of a chatbot leveraging large language models (LLMs) to bridge this gap. The chatbot interprets natural language questions about structured databases and translates them into SQL queries, retrieving relevant data and converting results back into natural language. The system was tested with four different LLMs, evaluated by human evaluation on a number of metrics: accuracy, relevance, fluency, completeness, cohesion, and naturalness. The results show the usefulness of the approach and how it can make data interaction more user-friendly by democratizing data access, reducing human workload, and empowering users to focus on strategic tasks.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3665939.3665968","title":["Causal Dataset Discovery with Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-3806-6844","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Junfei","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science, University of Rochester, Rochester, New York, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-4333-7858","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shaotong","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science, University of Rochester, Computer Science, New York, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4710-8719","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Fatemeh","family":"Nargesian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science, University of Rochester, Rochester, New York, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,14]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2024 Workshop on Human-In-the-Loop Data Analytics"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3665939.3665968"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5344814","title":["A Human-in-The-Loop Conceptual Design Framework Jointly Driven by Large Language Models and Knowledge Graphs"],"author":[{"given":"Yifan","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jian","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xin","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Haoran","family":"Cui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Minxia","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5344814"},{"DOI":"10.58332\/scirad2026v5i2a03","title":["Human-in-the-Loop Artificial Intelligence in Pharmaceutical Industry: Enhancing Efficiency and Maintaining Oversight"],"author":[{"given":"Seyyed Amir","family":"Siadati","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mohammad Ali","family":"Ebrahimzadeh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Akbar","family":"Dadras","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,17]]},"container-title":["Scientiae Radices"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.58332\/scirad2026v5i2a03","abstract":"<jats:p>Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming the pharmaceutical industry, offering unprecedented opportunities to accelerate drug discovery, optimize synthesis, and improve quality control. Despite its potential, unmonitored AI implementation carries significant risks, including errors in prediction, regulatory non-compliance, and ethical challenges. This perspective article explores the integration of AI across key stages of pharmaceutical research and development, including drug design, clinical trials, synthesis, analysis, and formulation, with an emphasis on human-in-the-loop supervision. We highlight areas where AI can deliver maximum efficiency gains and identify stages that require intensive human oversight to ensure safety, reliability, and regulatory adherence. By examining current applications and limitations, this article proposes a framework for combining AI capabilities with expert human judgment, aiming to enhance productivity while minimizing risk. Finally, we discuss future directions, including adaptive AI systems and hybrid models that promise to transform pharmaceutical operations without compromising ethical and scientific standards.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/systems14050455","title":["Humanizing ATS-Based Recruitment Using LLMs and Human-in-the-Loop Oversight"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-7155-8108","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Valdo V.","family":"Mpinga","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ESTG, Instituto Polit\u00e9cnico de Viana do Castelo, 4900-348 Viana do Castelo, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3883-1160","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ant\u00f3nio Miguel Rosado","family":"da Cruz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ADiT-Lab, Instituto Polit\u00e9cnico de Viana do Castelo, 4900-348 Viana do Castelo, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,22]]},"container-title":["Systems"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/systems14050455","abstract":"<jats:p>Application Tracking Systems (ATSs) have evolved significantly since their inception in 1996, transitioning from simple resum\u00e9repositories to AI-driven tools with advanced capabilities. While these developments have improved recruitment efficiency, they have also raised important ethical, organizational, and human-rights-related concerns. Bias in machine learning (ML) training data, opaque decision criteria, and excessive reliance on automated judgment may contribute to unfair treatment, reduced transparency, and limited human oversight in hiring processes. This study addresses these challenges by proposing a human-centered approach to ATS-supported recruitment based on a set of Humanization Services. Using a Design Science Research approach, three main artifacts were developed: a Job Requirements Validation Module, a Bias Trigger Removal Module, and a blockchain-supported dual-authorization mechanism for vacancy approval, which requires digital signatures from qualified professionals to approve job postings, ensuring that there are humans that assume responsibility. These components are intended to improve job posting quality, reduce bias-conducive information in applicant data, and strengthen accountability in recruitment workflows. The evaluation provides initial empirical support for the operational feasibility of the proposed approach under the tested conditions. The study therefore contributes a practical and theoretically grounded step toward more transparent, accountable, and human-centered AI-supported recruitment.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2214\/ajr.26.35340","title":["Diagnostic Reasoning Augmented by Large Language Models: Maintaining the Human-in-the-Loop"],"author":[{"given":"Gregory M.","family":"Lee","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Assistant Professor, Department of Radiology, University of K ansas Medical Center"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,17]]},"container-title":["American Journal of Roentgenology"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2214\/ajr.26.35340"},{"DOI":"10.51847\/qfrht9l78x","title":["Human-in-the-Loop Computational Phytopharmacology: Expert Curation, Model Oversight, Mechanistic Reasoning, and Translational Decision Confidence"],"author":[{"given":"Linus","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuki","family":"Sato","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Olivia","family":"Harris","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Pharmaceutical And Phytopharmacological Research"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.51847\/qfrht9l78x"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5417134","title":["Large Language Models as Simulated Human Investors: A Paradox of Model Sophistication"],"author":[{"given":"Ji","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yongxin","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Leping","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5417134"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0008953703440351","title":["An Architecture-independent Data Model for Managing Information Generated by Human-chatbot Interactions"],"author":[{"given":"Massimiliano","family":"Luca","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Information Engineering and Computer Science, University of Trento, Via Sommarive 5, Trento, Italy, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Alberto","family":"Montresor","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Information Engineering and Computer Science, University of Trento, Via Sommarive 5, Trento, Italy, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Carlo","family":"Caprini","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"U-Hopper S.r.l., Via R. da Sanseverino, 95, 38122, Trento, Italy, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Daniele","family":"Miorandi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"U-Hopper S.r.l., Via R. da Sanseverino, 95, 38122, Trento, Italy, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 8th International Conference on Model-Driven Engineering and Software Development"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0008953703440351"},{"DOI":"10.70619\/vol5iss4pp41-52","title":["Designing a Hybrid AI Chatbot Framework for Student Support: Integrating NLP and Human Oversight in African Universities"],"author":[{"given":"Denyse","family":"Bamurange","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dr. KN","family":"Jonathan, PhD","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,7]]},"container-title":["Journal of Information and Technology"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70619\/vol5iss4pp41-52","abstract":"<jats:p>The digital transformation of higher education in Africa necessitates innovative solutions tailored to the continent\u2019s linguistic diversity, cultural nuances, and infrastructural constraints. While AI chatbots offer promise in streamlining student support services, existing frameworks inadequately address challenges such as multilingual interactions, low-bandwidth environments, and compliance with evolving data regulations like Rwanda\u2019s Data Protection Law (No. 058\/2021). This study proposes a hybrid conceptual framework for AI chatbots that integrates lightweight Natural Language Processing (NLP) models with human oversight, designed specifically for African universities. By leveraging decision trees, intent mapping, and structured conversation flows, the framework enables institutions to automate routine tasks while maintaining contextual and empathetic support through dynamic escalation protocols. Key innovations include offline functionality for resource-constrained settings, cultural appropriateness checks to interpret indirect queries, and bias-mitigation strategies aligned with ethical guidelines. Developed through mixed-methods research, including case studies at the University of Kigali, expert interviews, and iterative prototyping, the framework demonstrated an 85% projected accuracy in resolving academic inquiries and reduced staff workload by 30% in simulations. Findings underscore the viability of no-code platforms for scalable deployment, emphasizing the balance between automation and human intervention. This research contributes a context-aware model for AI adoption in higher education, bridging global technological advancements with Africa\u2019s socio-technical realities while prioritizing ethical compliance and student-centric design.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/sys.70024","title":["Architecting Human\u2010AI Systems for Effective Collaboration and Oversight: Making Sense of Human\/AI\u2010in\/on\/Over\/Under\/Along\u2010the\u2010Loop"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5847-179X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Aditya","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"The George Washington University  Washington District of Columbia USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5601-1612","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zoe","family":"Szajnfarber","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The George Washington University  Washington District of Columbia USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,26]]},"container-title":["Systems Engineering"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/sys.70024","abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Governments around the world have written policies mandating human control over AI to ensure systems being deployed are safe and trustworthy. The core ideas behind these policies are that human control can prevent AI from making mistakes and that human collaboration with AI can leverage the unique strengths of both to create superior human\u2010AI systems. While the goals of these policies are laudable, they lack adequate explanations of how exactly humans are meant to have control over, or partner with, AI. To address this gap, we present a comprehensive taxonomy of human\u2010AI system architectures derived from synthesizing decades of human\u2010machine, human\u2010computer, and human\u2010robot interaction literature. Our taxonomy clarifies the conceptual inconsistencies of human\u2010in\/on\/etc.\u2010the\u2010loop terminology by analyzing prior literature through a common conceptual understanding of architecture, which we define as task allocation and the relationship between humans and AI. We identify critical gaps in existing frameworks, which have focused primarily on AI decision aids and human supervision, while neglecting emerging architectures. We also describe a four step process to use the taxonomy.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.31234\/osf.io\/3x7e8","title":["Evaluating the Efficacy of Amanda: A Voice-Based Large Language Model Chatbot for Relationship Challenges"],"author":[{"given":"Laura Marika","family":"Vowels","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Shannon","family":"Sweeney","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8811-1156","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Matthew J","family":"Vowels","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,22]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31234\/osf.io\/3x7e8","abstract":"<p>Digital health interventions are increasingly necessary to bridge gaps in mental health care, providing scalable and accessible solutions to address unmet needs. Relationship challenges, a significant driver of individual well-being and distress, are often under-supported due to barriers such as stigma, cost, and limited access to trained therapists. This study evaluates Amanda, a GPT-4-powered voice-based chatbot, designed to deliver single-session relationship support and enhance therapeutic engagement through natural and collaborative interactions. Participants (N = 54) completed a range of clinical outcome measures and their attitudes toward chatbots and digital health interventions pre- and post-intervention as well as two weeks later. In the interactions with the chatbot, the participants explored a range of relational issues and reported significant improvements in problem-specific outcomes, including reduced distress, enhanced communication, and greater confidence in managing conflicts directly after the interaction as well as two weeks later. While generic relationship outcomes showed only delayed improvements, individual well-being did not significantly change. Participants rated Amanda highly on usability, therapeutic skills, and working alliance, with reduced repetitiveness compared to the text-based version. These findings underscore the potential of voice-based chatbots to deliver accessible and effective relationship support. Future research should explore multi-session formats, clinical populations, and comparisons with other large language models to refine and expand AI-powered interventions.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1111\/all.15976","title":["Human\u2010in\u2010the\u2010loop: Human involvement in enhancing medical inquiry performance in large language models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7199-8371","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Linping","family":"Shu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery, Beijing TongRen Hospital Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Beijing Institute of Otolaryngology, Beijing Laboratory of Allergic Diseases, Beijing Key Laboratory of Nasal Diseases, Key Laboratory of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Ministry of Education Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Research Unit of Diagnosis and Treatment of Chronic Nasal Diseases Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences  Beijing China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4630-4056","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Qunshan","family":"He","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Control Science and Engineering Zhejiang University  Hangzhou PR China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2206-3750","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bing","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery, Beijing TongRen Hospital Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Beijing Institute of Otolaryngology, Beijing Laboratory of Allergic Diseases, Beijing Key Laboratory of Nasal Diseases, Key Laboratory of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Ministry of Education Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Research Unit of Diagnosis and Treatment of Chronic Nasal Diseases Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences  Beijing China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5742-5091","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Di","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery, Beijing TongRen Hospital Capital Medical University  Beijing China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3689-1584","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Menglin","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery, Beijing TongRen Hospital Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Beijing Institute of Otolaryngology, Beijing Laboratory of Allergic Diseases, Beijing Key Laboratory of Nasal Diseases, Key Laboratory of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Ministry of Education Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Research Unit of Diagnosis and Treatment of Chronic Nasal Diseases Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences  Beijing China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0646-5135","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chengshuo","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery, Beijing TongRen Hospital Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Beijing Institute of Otolaryngology, Beijing Laboratory of Allergic Diseases, Beijing Key Laboratory of Nasal Diseases, Key Laboratory of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Ministry of Education Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Research Unit of Diagnosis and Treatment of Chronic Nasal Diseases Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences  Beijing China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0910-9884","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Luo","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery, Beijing TongRen Hospital Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Beijing Institute of Otolaryngology, Beijing Laboratory of Allergic Diseases, Beijing Key Laboratory of Nasal Diseases, Key Laboratory of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Ministry of Education Capital Medical University  Beijing China"},{"name":"Research Unit of Diagnosis and Treatment of Chronic Nasal Diseases Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences  Beijing China"},{"name":"Department of Allergy, Beijing TongRen Hospital Capital Medical University  Beijing China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,12,11]]},"container-title":["Allergy"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1111\/all.15976"},{"DOI":"10.64898\/2026.05.03.26352241","title":["Multilingual Evaluation of a Large Language Model-Based Primary Care Chatbot"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-9061-3399","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pei-Lun","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"AI-4-AI Lab, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Amogh Ananda","family":"Rao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"AI-4-AI Lab, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sydney","family":"Pugh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"AI-4-AI Lab, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1016-9363","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kevin B","family":"Johnson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"AI-4-AI Lab, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,5]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64898\/2026.05.03.26352241","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>Pre-visit planning has the potential to reduce EHR documentation burden while improving workflow efficiency, care quality, and patient\u2013provider engagement. Large language model (LLM) chatbots show promise for supporting this task, but while their English-centric development suggests a potential for disparity, the extent to which these concerns translate into performance degradation in multilingual clinical settings remains unclear. In this mixed-methods study, we systematically evaluate the multilingual capabilities of PCP-Bot, an English-developed LLM-based (GPT-4o) clinical chatbot that collects patient concerns and generates structured, physician-ready summaries (\u223c200 words) under structured output constraints. We enrolled 31 bilingual individuals (11 Mandarin, 10 Spanish, 10 Hindi) to role-play as patients to evaluate the PCP-Bot, interacting with it across five synthetic clinical cases in both English and a second language. Participants completed a structured survey comprising baseline language proficiency screening, standardized interactions with PCP-Bot in each language, and post-interaction evaluations. Case order was randomized, with each scenario completed first in English and subsequently in the participant\u2019s second language. All summaries were generated in English, regardless of the interaction language. Our results show that Hindi achieved usability and conversation quality parity with English across all measured dimensions. Mandarin achieved usability parity but showed a significant conversation quality gap relative to English. Spanish demonstrated significant deficits in both conversation quality and summary quality. Trust and workload remained consistent across languages. Qualitatively, participants found PCP-Bot natural, smooth, and accurate overall, but noted repetition, transcription errors, missed follow-ups, and more frequent usability issues in non-English interactions. Overall, our findings demonstrate that LLM translation capabilities can enable effective deployment beyond English following appropriate performance validation.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.31234\/osf.io\/4gh7y","title":["Machine vs. Human, who makes better judgment? Take Large Language Model GPT-4 For Example (Version 2)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-8485-0311","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mark","family":"Du","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,4,12]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31234\/osf.io\/4gh7y","abstract":"<p>This essay explores the topic of human decision-making and the concept of noise, which refers to random and irrelevant factors that can affect decision-making. The essay argues that while humans are prone to noise in their decision-making processes, artificial intelligence (AI) can make less noise due to its ability to process large amounts of data and apply logical algorithms to make decisions. The essay examines examples and studies to demonstrate the impact of noise on human decision-making, including business ideas. Additionally, the essay highlights the potential that machine intuition is doing better than humans.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.51588\/cye9wr48","title":["Integrating Vector Control: A Large Language Model Chatbot for Interdisciplinary Design Processes"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5494-9540","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Otis Sloan","family":"Wood","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Royal Danish Academy \u2013 Architecture, Design, Conservation"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Farooq","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Prompt Engineering, Seattle"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,7]]},"container-title":["EAAE Joint Publishings"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.51588\/cye9wr48","abstract":"<jats:p>Working across disciplines is essential to tackle the complex global health challenges faced today. One such challenge is the proliferation of Aedes mosquitoes in recent decades, which has led to a significant and growing public health burden. According to the WHO, half of the world's population is at risk of contracting Aedes mosquito-borne viral diseases (MBVDs) such as dengue, and this is expected to rise as climate change expands the predicted geographic reach of these mosquitoes as far north as Denmark by 2050. There is currently limited vaccines or preventative therapeutics for these MBVDs, which means mosquito control remains the most viable approach to reduce them. Modifications to the built environment present numerous opportunities to reduce this disease burden, such as designing drainage that prevents stagnant water that can become larval sites or utilising screening to prevent mosquito entry. Significant advancements in vector control strategies have been made in the fields of public health and entomology; however, these have not effectively disseminated into the building profession. This paper explores the development and application of a proof-of-concept chatbot platform that utilises Large Language Models (LLMs) to bridge architecture and public health disciplines, aiding the integration of evidence-based mosquito control strategies into the early stages of building and landscape design. This LLM chatbot draws information from peer-reviewed articles from leading global health journals, identified in a previous literature review. Users can easily access information on vector control approaches for their projects by asking simple questions such as, \u2018How can I improve my entrance space to prevent mosquitoes?\u2019. By curating the LLM\u2019s source information, we aim to reduce AI hallucinations, where fictious generated information is presented as fact. This paper reflects upon the opportunities and challenges of LLM tools for interdisciplinary projects identified during the chatbots development.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.22214\/ijraset.2024.61195","title":["Gemini MultiPDF Chatbot: Multiple Document RAG Chatbot using Gemini Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Mohd","family":"Kaif","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sanskar","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dr. Sadhana","family":"Rana","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,5,31]]},"container-title":["International Journal for Research in Applied Science and Engineering Technology"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22214\/ijraset.2024.61195","abstract":"<jats:p>Abstract: The Gemini MultiPDF Chatbot represents a groundbreaking advancement in natural language processing (NLP) by integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) techniques with the Gemini Large Language Model. This innovative chatbot is designed to handle multiple document retrieval and generation tasks, leveraging the extensive knowledge base of the Gemini model. By harnessing RAG methods, the chatbot enhances its ability to acquire, comprehend, and generate responses across diverse knowledge sources contained within multiple PDF documents. The integration of Gemini's powerful language understanding capabilities with RAG facilitates seamless interaction with users, offering comprehensive and contextually relevant responses. This paper presents the design, implementation, and evaluation of the Gemini MultiPDF Chatbot, demonstrating its effectiveness in navigating complex information landscapes and delivering high-quality conversational experiences.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s44260-026-00075-1","title":["Too human to model: the uncanny valley of large language models in simulating human systems"],"author":[{"given":"Yongchao","family":"Zeng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Calum","family":"Brown","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mark","family":"Rounsevell","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,2]]},"container-title":["npj Complexity"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s44260-026-00075-1","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) have been increasingly used to simulate human behaviour because of their ability to generate contextually coherent dialogues. Such abilities can enhance the realism of models. However, the pursuit of realism is not necessarily compatible with the epistemic foundation of modelling. We explore when LLM agents can be too \u2018human\u2019 to model, i.e., when they are too expressive, detailed and intractable to be consistent with the abstraction, simplification, and interpretability typically demanded by modelling. Through a model-building thought experiment, we uncover five core dilemmas: a temporal resolution mismatch between natural conversation and abstract time steps; the need for intervention in agent conversations without undermining spontaneous outputs; the temptation to introduce rules while maintaining conversational naturalness; the tension between role consistency and role evolution; and the challenge of understanding emergence. These dilemmas lead LLM agents to an \u201cuncanny valley\u201d: more realistic than rule-based agents but recognisably unhuman.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1101\/2023.01.27.23285115","title":["Analysis of large-language model versus human performance for genetics questions"],"author":[{"given":"Dat","family":"Duong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Benjamin D.","family":"Solomon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,1,28]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1101\/2023.01.27.23285115","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>Large-language models like ChatGPT have recently received a great deal of attention. To assess ChatGPT in the field of genetics, we compared its performance to human respondents in answering genetics questions (involving 13,636 responses) that had been posted on social media platforms starting in 2021. Overall, ChatGPT did not perform significantly differently than human respondents, but did significantly better on memorization-type questions versus critical thinking questions, frequently provided different answers when asked questions multiple times, and provided plausible explanations for both correct and incorrect answers.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4683054","title":["Revolutionizing Marketing Research with a Large Language Model: A Hybrid AI-Human Approach"],"author":[{"given":"Neeraj","family":"Arora","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7210-2773","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ishita","family":"Chakraborty","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yohei","family":"Nishimura","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4683054","abstract":"<jats:p>An area within marketing that is well poised for adoption of large language models (LLMs) is marketing research. In this paper the authors empirically investigate how LLMs could potentially assist at different stages of the marketing research process. They partnered with a Fortune 500 food company to replicate a qualitative and quantitative study conducted by the company in 2019 using GPT-4. The authors designed the system architecture and prompts necessary to create personas, ask questions, and obtain answers from synthetic respondents. For qualitative research, the LLMs demonstrated a good capability in data generation through synthetic respondents and moderation, producing information-rich, coherent data that humans found to be superior in depth and informativeness. The LLMs also matched human performance in basic data analysis tasks, such as generating themes and summaries. For quantitative research, the foundation LLM (GPT-4) correctly picked the answer direction and valence. Incorporating context (few-shot learning and retrieval-augmented generation) significantly improved the quality of synthetic data for quantitative research. The paper lays out road-maps for incorporating LLMs in marketing research and concludes that they can serve as an excellent collaborator\/assistant for marketing researchers.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1111\/1748-8583.70036","title":["On the Dangers of Large\u2010Language Model Mediated Learning for Human Capital"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5155-4359","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dirk","family":"Lindebaum","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Bath School of Management  Bath UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Eimear","family":"Nolan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Trinity Business School Trinity College Dublin  Dublin Ireland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Juani","family":"Swart","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Bath School of Management  Bath UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,17]]},"container-title":["Human Resource Management Journal"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1111\/1748-8583.70036","abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Against the dominant view in HRM concerning the value\u2010creating use of large language models (LLMs) in relation to Human Capital, our provocation asks whether LLMs will enhance or compromise Human Capital at work in the long\u2010run. We feel compelled to ask this question because Human Capital represents employees' accumulated learning experiences, which provide the knowledge and skills needed to perform effectively at work. However, knowledge is a multifaceted rather than monolithic phenomenon, requiring a more differentiated treatment when considering the use of LLMs at work, its effects on different types of knowledge and, eventually, the formation of Human Capital. We mobilise digitally mediated learning\u2014where synthetic inputs replace first\u2010hand experience\u2014to theorise mechanisms for explaining how LLMs (as one Gen\u2010AI application producing synthetic content) shape different types of knowledge, and the formation of Human Capital. We identify two mechanisms, namely, (i) multiple degrees of abstraction from the concrete real\u2010world to the digital world and (ii) the conflation of \u2018word form\u2019 and \u2018meaning\u2019 in outputs of LLMs. We consider the theoretical and practical ramifications of our provocation for the development of Human Capital in the age of LLMs.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4135\/9788132106005.n12","title":["National Human Rights Commission and Police Oversight"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2010]]},"container-title":["Enforcing Police Accountability through Civilian Oversight"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4135\/9788132106005.n12"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3770761.3777116","title":["Edison 3.0: A Multimodal RAG System for Large-Scale Educational Q&amp;A with Human-in-the-Loop Oversight"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8229-0301","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Meenakshi","family":"Mittal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Berkeley, Berkeley, California, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-5746-1791","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rishi","family":"Khare","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Berkeley, Berkeley, California, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-6338-6671","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mihran","family":"Miroyan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Berkeley, Berkeley, California, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-9826-7534","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chancharik","family":"Mitra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9861-7540","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Narges","family":"Norouzi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Berkeley, Berkeley, California, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,17]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 57th ACM Technical Symposium on Computer Science Education V.2"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3770761.3777116"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icsa-c68850.2026.00055","title":["Human-in-the-Loop in MLOps: A Systematic Review on Balancing Automation and Human Oversight"],"author":[{"given":"Louisa","family":"Kl\u00fcter","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam","place":["Amsterdam, the Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"D\u00f3ra","family":"Kementzey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam","place":["Amsterdam, the Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Faezeh Amou","family":"Najafabadi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam","place":["Amsterdam, the Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Keerthiga","family":"Rajenthiram","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam","place":["Amsterdam, the Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ilias","family":"Gerostathopoulos","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam","place":["Amsterdam, the Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"container-title":["2026 IEEE 23rd International Conference on Software Architecture Companion (ICSA-C)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icsa-c68850.2026.00055"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009635622.006","title":["Language, Social Structures, Processes, Relations, and Meaning"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]]},"container-title":["Ideology and the Language of Human Rights"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009635622.006"},{"DOI":"10.1093\/acamed\/wvag080","title":["Preserving the human in the loop: defining appropriate supervision of large language models in academic medicine"],"author":[{"given":"Aaron W","family":"Calhoun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Critical Care, University of Louisville School of Medicine and Norton Children\u2019s Medical Group , Louisville, KY,","place":["United States"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nicole","family":"Harder","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Nursing, University of Manitoba , Winnipeg, Manitoba,","place":["Canada"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,23]]},"container-title":["Academic Medicine"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/acamed\/wvag080"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/sta4.70060","title":["Does a Large Language Model Really Speak in Human\u2010Like Language?"],"author":[{"given":"Mose","family":"Park","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Statistical Data Science University of Seoul  Seoul South Korea"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0194-5802","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yunjin","family":"Choi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Statistics University of Seoul  Seoul South\u00a0Korea"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jong\u2010June","family":"Jeon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Statistics University of Seoul  Seoul South\u00a0Korea"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,18]]},"container-title":["Stat"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/sta4.70060","abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) have recently emerged, attracting considerable attention due to their ability to generate highly natural, human\u2010like text. This study compares the latent community structures of LLM\u2010generated text and human\u2010written text within a hypothesis testing procedure. Specifically, we analyse three text sets: original human\u2010written texts (), their LLM\u2010paraphrased versions () and a twice\u2010paraphrased set () derived from . Our analysis addresses two key questions: (1) Is the difference in latent community structures between  and  the same as that between  and ? (2) Does  become more similar to  as the LLM parameter controlling text variability is adjusted? The first question is based on the assumption that if LLM\u2010generated text truly resembles human language, then the gap between the pair () should be similar to that between the pair (), as both pairs consist of an original text and its paraphrase. The second question examines whether the degree of similarity between LLM\u2010generated and human text varies with changes in the breadth of text generation. To address these questions, we propose a statistical hypothesis testing framework that leverages the fact that each text has corresponding parts across  and . This relationship enables the mapping of one dataset's relative position to another, allowing two datasets to be mapped to a third dataset. As a result, both mapped datasets can be quantified with respect to the space characterized by the third dataset, facilitating a direct comparison between them. For , the original human text, we collected customer reviews from an accommodation booking site; for  and , we used GPT\u20103.5 to paraphrase . Our results indicate that GPT\u2010generated text remains distinct from human\u2010authored text.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.35542\/osf.io\/azhf9","title":["A prototype of a chatbot for evaluating and refining student startup ideas using a large language model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3748-6468","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Joseph Benjamin Rodriguez","family":"Ilagan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jose Ramon","family":"Ilagan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,6,3]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.35542\/osf.io\/azhf9","abstract":"<p>Assessing the soundness of business models is a critical skill for aspiring entrepreneurs and is an essential part of entrepreneurship education. However, evaluating business models can be time-consuming, costly, and subjective. This study describes the design and the prototype of a chatbot as a conversational intelligent tutoring system that assesses and gives feedback on business model soundness using natural language processing techniques and GPT-3.5, a large language model (LLM) trained by OpenAI, to help student co-founders learn and refine their startup ideas. Our method involves indexing articles and rubrics for evaluating technology startup pitches by extracting word embeddings via the OpenAI API. The chatbot accepts descriptions of startup businesses from student co-founders through a Telegram chatbot, and these are formatted as prompts and then fed into GPT-3.5. The responses are formulated by GPT-3 based on another set of prompts instructing the bot to give feedback from three virtual panelists: 1) a harsh judge, 2) a neutral expert, and 3) an optimistic investor.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7202605","title":["The MIRROR Model: Multi-Model Iterative Reflection, Review, Oversight, and Reconciliation A Human-Supervised Protocol for Transparent, Evidence-Grounded Knowledge Work"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8416-2300","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Hamed","family":"Yazdanshenas","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7202605","abstract":"<jats:p>Importance: General-purpose large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used for research, writing, interpretation, and decision preparation. Yet a fluent answer from a single model may contain factual errors, omit relevant evidence, reflect prompt framing or user preference, reproduce shared blind spots, and convey unwarranted confidence. Existing methods address parts of this problem through self-refinement, multi-agent debate, role prompting, self-consistency, retrieval, and factual verification, but these components are rarely integrated into a transparent end-user workflow with explicit human accountability. Objective: To introduce the MIRROR Model, define its conceptual and operational basis, provide a reproducible implementation workflow, and specify a prospective program for evaluating reliability, efficiency, and human-factors consequences. Design: Foundational methods paper and proposed empirical-validation protocol informed by research on iterative refinement, multi-agent deliberation, role prompting, hallucination detection, evaluator bias, sycophancy, and human-AI decision support. The MIRROR Model is implemented through Stage 0 task triage followed by eight core stages. Proposed Evaluation Measures: Primary outcomes are expert-rated factual accuracy, completeness, calibration, reasoning quality, and decision usefulness. Secondary outcomes include unsupported-claim density, correction yield, false-correction rate, source-verification accuracy, inter-rater reliability, multi-model diversity, residual disagreement, factual improvement per 1,000 generated tokens, time, cost, cost-adjusted benefit, and user overreliance. Prespecified alternative workflows include single-pass prompting, same-model self-refinement, structured single-model checklists, unblinded multi-model review, retrieval-grounded single-model response, direct primary-source lookup, and the full MIRROR workflow. Conclusions and Relevance: The MIRROR Model converts an informal practice-asking distinct AI systems to generate, critique, revise, verify, and synthesize answers-into a transparent and auditable human-supervised methodology. Its contribution lies in the integration, ordering, safeguards, and documentation of established components rather than in a claim that critique, debate, role prompting, reframing, or verification are individually new. MIRROR is not a guarantee of correctness and does not replace external evidence or qualified professional judgment. Prospective, blinded, and independently replicated studies are required before superiority over alternative workflows can be claimed.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.eswa.2024.125861","title":["LLM-TSFD: An industrial time series human-in-the-loop fault diagnosis method based on a large language model"],"author":[{"given":"Qi","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chao","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yicheng","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinsong","family":"Bao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dan","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3]]},"container-title":["Expert Systems with Applications"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.eswa.2024.125861"},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9193113\/v1","title":["Hebbian Inertia and Massless Reasoning: Comparative Cognitive Architecture in Human and Large Language Model Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Emary","family":"Iacobucci","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University at Buffalo, State University of New York"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Joseph","family":"Woelfel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University at Buffalo, State University of New York"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,5]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9193113\/v1","abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Purpose\n Human societies and artificial intelligence models learn in fundamentally different ways. Humans update beliefs through repeated exposure, a Hebbian process that creates cognitive \u201cinertia\u201d: once reinforced through experience, beliefs resist change. Large language models (LLMs), by contrast, do not update their parameters at inference; they pivot instantly between perspectives, unburdened by cumulative experience. Despite growing interest in comparing human and machine cognition, no prior study has placed both on a shared measurement scale that permits direct quantitative comparison. This paper reports the first such comparison, examining whether the architectural differences between human and LLM cognition produce measurably distinct signatures in belief structure and change.\nDesign\/methodology\/approach\n We employ the Galileo Method, a multidimensional scaling technique that derives spatial representations of concepts from paired-comparison judgments of dissimilarity. Unlike conventional approaches that impose Euclidean geometry on psychological data, the Galileo Method preserves measured distances in their native metric, allowing non-Euclidean structures to emerge when warranted by the data. Participants - both human respondents and three LLM systems (Claude, DeepSeek, and ChatGPT-5) - provided dissimilarity judgments for identical concept sets. These judgments were then projected into coordinate spaces, enabling comparison of distances, clustering patterns, and response to belief-challenging information across agent types.\nFindings\n Human respondents display inertial pseudo-Riemannian signatures consistent with Hebbian learning: concepts reinforced through experience occupy stable positions that resist perturbation. LLMs, by contrast, exhibit what we term \u201cmassless\u201d reasoning dynamics, repositioning concepts fluidly without the friction imposed by prior reinforcement, all arrayed in Euclidean space. These patterns held across all three LLM systems tested, suggesting they reflect architectural properties of transformer-based models rather than idiosyncrasies of particular implementations.\nResearch implications\n The results point toward a research program in comparative cognitive architecture. By placing human and machine cognition on shared measurement frameworks, researchers can move beyond impressionistic comparisons to systematic analysis of how different learning architectures shape the structure and malleability of belief.\nOriginality\/value\n This study provides the first direct human\u2013LLM comparison using a shared, geometrically unconstrained measurement scale; demonstrates that Hebbian inertia leaves detectable signatures absent in LLM cognition; and establishes a methodological template for future comparative work.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.64643\/ijirtv12i10-194028-459","title":["To know About Human Behavior using Large Language Model"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,12]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Innovative Research in Technology"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64643\/ijirtv12i10-194028-459"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7130558","title":["&lt;p&gt;Extreme Human Behavior and Escalation Dynamics: Toward a General Theory of Behavioral Escalation Within the Latent Dynamics Model&lt;\/p&gt;"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-7387-3943","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Sergio","family":"Pommier Gallo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7130558","abstract":"<jats:p>This paper examines extreme human behavior through the analytical framework of the Latent Dynamics Model (LDM). Traditional criminological explanations frequently attribute extreme criminality to isolated variables such as psychopathology, antisocial personality traits, social deprivation, ideological radicalization, neurological abnormalities, or environmental influences. Although such approaches contribute valuable insights, they often fail to explain how individuals progressively evolve from relatively ordinary forms of deviant behavior toward increasingly severe manifestations of criminal conduct. This study proposes a General Theory of Behavioral Escalation derived from the Latent Dynamics Model. The theory argues that extreme criminality emerges through dynamic interactions among neurocognitive predispositions, environmental pressures, reinforcement mechanisms, adaptive learning processes, opportunity structures, and behavioral feedback systems. Through qualitative doctrinal analysis integrating criminology, forensic psychology, neurocriminology, behavioral science, and criminal law theory, the paper develops a five-stage escalation framework capable of explaining the emergence, stabilization, and amplification of harmful conduct. The findings suggest that extreme criminal behavior frequently represents the endpoint of prolonged escalation trajectories rather than the product of sudden transformation. The study concludes that the Latent Dynamics Model provides a comprehensive framework for understanding behavioral intensification, criminal persistence, and the development of extreme forms of offending within contemporary criminogenic environments.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.14195\/2183-203x_23_4","title":["Human capital and corruption: a microeconomic model of the bribes market with democratic contestability"],"author":[{"given":"Pedro Cosme","family":"Costa Vieira","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Aurora A. C.","family":"Teixeira","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2006,6,22]]},"container-title":["Notas Econ\u00f3micas"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14195\/2183-203x_23_4","abstract":"<jats:p>To overcome market failures society creates common laws that stimulate or penalize individual actions, the enforcement of which depends on the actions of public authorities who may be susceptible to corruption. We model this behaviour for an autocracy versus a democracy, using a microeconomic framework. We assume that in an autocracy rulers have a monopoly over the bribes market, whereas in a democracy conflicting groups compete in the bribes market. The models constructed produce results that are compatible with the well-known stylized facts, namely that (1) in a democracy the level of corruption is lower than in an autocracy, although still positive, that (2) in environments where the level of human capital is higher, regimes are closer to democracies and the level of corruption is lower, and that (3) the level of corruption is higher in more regulated economies.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.26502\/fjhs.244","title":["A Chatbot that Learns One\u2019s Preferences as the Next Step in Human Digital Twins: A Pilot Study Using HyperCLOVA X, a Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Joseph","family":"Yun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Joshua","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yohan","family":"Yun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Stanley","family":"Yoon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sang-Hoon","family":"Park","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sijung","family":"Yun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Fortune Journal of Health Sciences"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26502\/fjhs.244"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11390936","title":["Interactive LLMs with Human-in-the-Loop for Ethical Content Generation"],"author":[{"given":"Yazan","family":"Al Ahmed","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Al Ain University,Faculty of Engineering,Abu Dhabi,UAE"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Salsabeel","family":"Naser","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Al Ain University,Faculty of Engineering,Abu Dhabi,UAE"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Abrar","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Al Ain University,Faculty of Engineering,Abu Dhabi,UAE"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"container-title":["2025 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11390936"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/fie63693.2025.11328405","title":["Leveraging Large Language Models and Human-In-The-Loop for Interactive Learning Pipelines"],"author":[{"given":"Haoze","family":"Du","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"North Carolina State University,Raleigh,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dinesh","family":"Kannan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"North Carolina State University,Raleigh,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bhavishya","family":"Tarun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"North Carolina State University,Raleigh,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Edward F.","family":"Gehringer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"North Carolina State University,Raleigh,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,2]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE Frontiers in Education Conference (FIE)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/fie63693.2025.11328405"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4658623","title":["Post-Deployment Regulatory Oversight for General-Purpose Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7488-9258","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Carson","family":"Ezell","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abraham","family":"Loeb","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4658623","abstract":"<jats:p>The development and deployment of increasingly capable, general-purpose large language models (LLMs) has led to a wide array of risks and harms from automation that are correlated across sectors and use cases. Effective regulation and oversight of general-purpose AI (GPAI) requires the ability to monitor, investigate, and respond to risks and harms that appear across use cases, as well as hold upstream developers accountable for downstream harms that result from their decisions and practices. We argue that existing processes for sector-specific AI oversight in the U.S. should be complemented by post-deployment oversight to address risks and harms specifically from GPAI usage. We examine oversight processes implemented by other federal agencies as precedents for the GPAI oversight activities that a regulatory agency can conduct. The post-deployment oversight function of a regulatory agency can complement other GPAI-related regulatory functions that federal regulatory agencies may perform which are discussed elsewhere in the literature, including pre-deployment licensing or model evaluations for LLMs.<\/jats:p>"}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}